WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:02.960
- [Angelo] You think they
ready to sing, Dirty Walt?

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:04.069
- [Walter] Yeah, let's see.

00:00:04.069 --> 00:00:05.499
- [Angelo] You think
they ready to sing, Jay?

00:00:05.499 --> 00:00:07.206
- [Walter] Let's get
that sunshine shit goin'!

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:09.120
- [Angelo] Come on, help me out now!

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.547
- [Walter] Come on, sing it!

00:00:10.547 --> 00:00:13.248
♫ Everyday sunshine

00:00:13.248 --> 00:00:15.571
♫ Everyday sunshine

00:00:15.571 --> 00:00:17.461
- Back then, we’d get a lot of

00:00:17.461 --> 00:00:19.432
guys playing that white boy music,

00:00:19.432 --> 00:00:20.851
and we really don't listen to that,

00:00:20.851 --> 00:00:22.860
you know, and it's going too fast.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.581
- To finally get to a
place where there is now

00:00:25.581 --> 00:00:29.221
a sea of all the rainbows of people,

00:00:29.221 --> 00:00:32.080
but a lot of black kids there.

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:35.230
We're at the point of progress.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:37.082
- [Angelo] I hear from
a lot of people of color

00:00:37.082 --> 00:00:38.383
say you guys made it okay

00:00:38.383 --> 00:00:41.714
for me to feel like it was
alright to be black and rock.

00:00:41.714 --> 00:00:43.214
I hear that a lot.

00:00:44.655 --> 00:00:46.885
- [Narrator] Brooklyn's
Afropunk Music Festival

00:00:46.885 --> 00:00:50.802
was born in 2005 for
alternative black culture.

00:00:51.674 --> 00:00:54.594
But before there was a
space for celebration,

00:00:54.594 --> 00:00:56.712
a strong handful of bands
were paving the way,

00:00:56.712 --> 00:00:58.629
like the band Fishbone.

00:01:00.143 --> 00:01:01.623
- [Angelo] My name is Angelo Moore,

00:01:01.623 --> 00:01:03.456
frontman of Fishbone.

00:01:05.015 --> 00:01:06.943
- [John] I'm John Norwood Fisher,

00:01:06.943 --> 00:01:08.693
bassist for Fishbone.

00:01:11.013 --> 00:01:14.725
Fishbone was formed in the late 70's,

00:01:14.725 --> 00:01:17.142
and it was Los Angeles, 1978.

00:01:18.854 --> 00:01:20.783
- [Angelo] Weren't too many
bands that looked like us,

00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:24.085
because all black bands
playing punk rock and ska,

00:01:24.085 --> 00:01:26.335
probably count it on one hand.

00:01:26.335 --> 00:01:28.585
♫ Sunshine

00:01:30.813 --> 00:01:32.813
- The crazy part about it was,

00:01:32.813 --> 00:01:34.445
while we were starting to play a lot

00:01:34.445 --> 00:01:36.317
of rock and roll, we really weren't

00:01:36.317 --> 00:01:39.167
embraced too much by the black public.

00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:41.189
We were embraced a lot
by the white public,

00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:43.816
so we’d get a lot of white
kids coming to the show.

00:01:43.816 --> 00:01:45.565
Black people ain't supposed to play rock.

00:01:45.565 --> 00:01:47.119
That's not the stereotype.

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:51.377
Black people are supposed to
play R&amp;B and funk and soul.

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:54.487
If you got dreadlocks, you
supposed to be playing reggae.

00:01:54.487 --> 00:01:56.567
- If you put on a suit
and tie, you play jazz.

00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:58.039
- You can play jazz, right?

00:01:58.039 --> 00:02:00.367
(laughter)

00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:04.287
Afropunk is a celebration
of rock and roll,

00:02:04.287 --> 00:02:06.370
black music for everyone.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:14.262
- [John] It's incredible and
it draws in youth of color

00:02:14.262 --> 00:02:18.429
to say this is for you in
a larger way than we did.

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:23.837
- [Angelo] You can be the
beautiful black you, and rock.

